Once you earn a score, it's yours to keep.  The game has enough fun playing
with ghost levels, score files, and so on, to recall which ghost killed you.
I believe that the ghost level just records the equipment and the HP of the
dead character, and not the position the dying character earned in the
logfile and record files.

I'll think I'll take the moment to throw in $0.02 to the Quit/Don't Quit
controversy.

Certainly, when one finds himself actually debating whether a situation
is survivable or not, they should consider what sort of bones file is
being left behind.  If the ghost file is created, chances are something
like 2 in 3, that the next visitor to that level will get the ghost file.
One should consider how likely a typical character will survive the level.

Situations that make for potentially deadly ghost levels:

1) Purple worms roaming freely above Dungeon Level 5 (I've seen them on
   Level 2 a few times, usually summoned by Shrieker Mushrooms).  Most
   starting characters don't have much a chance against these.

2) Raiding shops with a character name you love to play repeatedly.
   I;m too lazy to change my name every time I play the game, and
   if you rob the shop in one game, and then die, the shopkeeper will
   fight you every time he sees you.

3) Carrying powerful weapons while surrounded by humanoids.  Even artifacts
   won't help when you're fainting with a displeased God.  If you die right
   there, there's the possibility that a humanoid could pick up the weapon
   on the bones level.  I don't know about you, but I wouldn't relish
   being on the receiving end of Mjollnir.

Sometimes it would just be nice to quit just to nuke the bones level.
If I have the mechanics right, the bones file is unlinked when you visit
it, and then created if you die.  If you strategically Quit while on a
particularly nasty bones level, then maybe your Next character will have
an easier time getting through the dungeon.

In short, I usually favor playing over quitting, since I'm an incurable
optimist.  However, once in awhile, Quitting Is (IMHO) a better move.

